USAGE = """\
usage: scripts/tests/compare --binary <blaze> [--oracle <bazel>] [--case ID]... [--label NAME]
                             [--skip-oracle]
       scripts/tests/compare --oracle-only [--oracle <bazel>] [--case ID]... [--label NAME]
       scripts/tests/compare list
       scripts/tests/compare selftest
       scripts/tests/compare help

Runs a fixed list of `test` (and `build`) invocations (see `list`) with the oracle and with
Blaze, each side on its own work copy of the corpus project re2
(~/.cache/blaze/work/tests-compare/<side>/re2: rsync of the read-only checkout minus bazel-* and
.git, restored to the checkout's git HEAD, plus the probe package scripts/tests/probe as
//blaze_tests_probe) and its own output user root (~/.cache/blaze/oracle-out/tests-compare-<side>),
with a clean environment (HOME, PATH=/usr/bin:/bin), --nohome_rc --nosystem_rc, the shared
repository cache, --color=no --curses=no and a build event JSON file per case, under
systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=12G -p MemorySwapMax=0. Both sides run the same cases in
the same order from a fresh output root; each case starts with `clean` so no result is cached.

Compared per case:
  termination  exit code, or signal, OOM kill, timeout
  stdout       in full (--test_output=streamed writes the test logs there)
  stderr       in full
  ran          the test results of the build event stream (label, run, shard, attempt, status),
               sorted, except for cases marked ordered (exclusive tests) where the stream order
               is compared
stdout and stderr after the exclusion rules of scripts/lib/compare_common.py (VOLATILE_RULES;
`paths`: each side's workspace copy, output user root and output base; `durations`: every
"N.Ns" duration; B1_GAP_RULES: lines that depend on the oracle's warm server, which `clean`
does not stop: the server-start line, packages loaded, the process summary, the position of
"INFO: Found ..."), listed in the report with the lines each changed or removed on each side.

Guards (scripts/lib/compare_common.py): every command runs under a supervisor in the scope that
records how it really ended (`exit N`, `signal NAME`, `oom-kill`, `timeout`); `exit` compares
those (a record without a termination never matches). --oracle must be the pinned oracle
(UPSTREAM.json). An empty selection is an error. The terminations of `clean` and `shutdown` are
listed (an abnormal one fails the run). Classes: IDENTICAL (all of it equal after the volatile
rules; the oracle exited 0, or DECLARED_EXIT names the case with that exit code), KNOWN-GAP
(equal except in B1 lines), DIFFERENT, BOTH-FAILED (equal
failures not declared), NOT-PORTED, ORACLE-UNEXPECTED, SKIPPED.

Output: a header (repository HEAD and dirty flag, both binaries' paths and sha256), a table, the
denominator; raw and normalised outputs under
~/.cache/blaze/tests-compare/<label>/<side>/<case>.{stdout,stderr,bep.json,norm.json}, and
results.json (with provenance and denominator). Exit 0 only when at least one case was compared
and every selected case is IDENTICAL; 1 otherwise; 2 on a usage error or a refusal.
--skip-oracle reuses the oracle outputs of an earlier run with the same --label only when
<label>/oracle/oracle.key, written after those outputs were complete, holds this run's key
(oracle sha256, every argv and the environment, the digests of the re2 checkout and of the
probe, the case sequence) and every file it anchors is unchanged; otherwise it refuses. The
files are raw (normalised when compared), so this tool's sha256 is not in the key.
--oracle-only records them without running Blaze (nothing judged: exit 1 at best).
selftest drives this command line with fake binaries (compare_common.selftest_tool).
"""

